Q: What is dirty checking in Hibernate?
	Dirty checking is the mechanism by which Hibernate automatically detects changes made to a managed entity and generates the required SQL UPDATE during transaction flush/commit.

	Example:
		@Transactional
		public void updateUser(Long id) {

			User user = userRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();

			user.setName("Rahul");						// Hibernate can detect that the managed user changed
		}

		userRepository.save(user);				// We don't explicitly call:

		Hibernate can detect that the managed User changed and generate:
			UPDATE user
			SET name = 'Rahul'
			WHERE id = ?;

		How does it work?
			find entity
				↓
			Entity becomes managed
				↓
			Hibernate keeps original state
				↓
			Application modifies entity
				↓
			Hibernate detects difference
				↓
			Flush
				↓
			UPDATE SQL

	To achieve this :
		1. Entity Must Be Managed
			Customer customer = customerRepository.findById(id).get();					// new Customer(); will not work
			customer.setName("New Name");

		2. @Transactional			// required


"Hibernate maintains the original state of managed entities. During flush, it compares the current state with the original state. If it detects a change,
it generates the required SQL update. This is called dirty checking."
