Q: What is CORS ?
	CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser security mechanism that controls whether a web page from one origin can access resources from another origin.
	An Origin is defined by:
		Protocol + Domain + Port

	http://localhost:3000
	http://localhost:8080		// have different ports, so they are considered different origins.

	Suppose your frontend is running on:			http://localhost:3000
	and your Spring Boot backend is running on:		http://localhost:8080

	Frontend makes a request:	fetch("http://localhost:8080/customers")
	Browser blocks it:		Access to fetch has been blocked by CORS policy. 			// because it's a cross-origin request.

	How CORS Works :
		Browser Request -
			Frontend
			http://localhost:3000
					↓
			GET /customers
					↓
			Backend
			http://localhost:8080

		Backend Response -
			Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:3000


		Browser checks this header.
			If allowed:	✅ Response returned

			Otherwise:	❌ Request blocked

		# Spring Boot Example :
		Controller Level
			@RestController
			@CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:3000")					// Only Frontend app running on port 3000 can access this API.
			public class CustomerController {

				@GetMapping("/customers")
				public List<Customer> getCustomers() {
					return List.of();
				}
			}

		# Global Configuration :
		@Configuration
		public class CorsConfig {

			@Bean
			public WebMvcConfigurer corsConfigurer() {

				return new WebMvcConfigurer() {

					@Override
					public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {

						registry.addMapping("/**")
								.allowedOrigins("http://localhost:3000")
								.allowedMethods("*");
					}
				};
			}
		}

		Common CORS HTTP Headers:
			Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:3000
			Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET,POST,PUT,DELETE
			Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type
			Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
